  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">CED-Support deaf people</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">In collaboration with VUJUSAP of Ho Chi Minh city, Center for Research and Education of the deaf and hard of hearing proposed the project &#x201c;Let people with hard of hearing&#x2019;s voices heard&#x201d;, which is considered the second phase of its initiative implementedin JIFF&#x2019;s2nd Grant Call. As such, End-of-project products, including medical sign language, handbook to support the deaf, handbook of consultation process and legal advice, a website for teaching sign language and so on will be in great use for micro-scale activities implemented in the community (in particular, education and awareness-raising communications). Results and lessons learned then will be recorded in the Handbook &#x201c;Let the Deaf Be Heard&#x201d;. For the long-term goal, Deaf people have better access to law and justice while resources in the community are mobilized to promote the more effective application of state policies on public health into real-life conditions. Accordingly, project activities are designed on the fundamental of 3 specific outcomes: (1) Raising public awareness on matters related to people with hard of hearing; (2) People with hard of hearing have better access to legal aid services and (3) Contributing comments to the development of policies related to people with disabilities. Although the focus group is the whole communityof people with disabilities, the involvement of women in this group is slightly highly encouraged. In addition, Hospital of obstetrics and gynecology is also opted for piloting the supporting models for peoplewith hard of hearing in need. At the end of the project, the total number of beneficiaries is expected to be 1910, with 480 people with hard of hearing, 200 children; 100 women in pregnancy and/or with children; 100 over-60 elderlies; 1030 people workingand/or doing care work with people with hearing impairment (suchas parents, teachers, interpreters, social workers, health care staff,...).</narrative>
  </description>
  <participating-org role="4" type="80">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">&apos;Center for Research and Education of the Deaf and Hard of Hearing (CED) operates on:
- Conducting research on disabilities, especially hearing disabilities.
- Defending and empowering the deaf and hard of hearing to help them integrate into society.
- Raising community awareness to change people#s attitude towards the deaf, painting a positive image of the community of deaf and hard of hearing.
- Creating favorable conditions for these people in accessing education, employment, and basic public services.
- Unleashing their potential and talent of the deaf.</narrative>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Center for Research and Education o</narrative>
